<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pharnavaz I of Iberia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 300 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pharnavaz I was the first king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, or Kartli, in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, according to the medieval Georgian chronicles, which place him around 300 BCE. They credit him with uniting the country after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, organising it into provinces under eristavis and creating the Georgian script, a claim modern scholars reject. His historicity is uncertain, but he became a founding figure of Georgian kingship.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pharnavaz-i-of-iberia.jpg" alt="Pharnavaz I of Iberia" width="320" height="694" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jaqeli · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pharnavaz_I_relief_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
